# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
mill-proof.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: mill-proof.logic.r2 has "desires_own → own_good"; mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3 have "desires_own → desirable_capable"; they differ when only "each person, so far as he believes it attainable, desires his own happiness" and "each person's happiness is a good to that person (desirable in the sense of worthy of desire)" are true, which the first allows
mill-proof.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "each person, so far as he believes it attainable, desires his own happiness" and "each person's happiness is a good to that person (desirable in the sense of worthy of desire)" are true and "happiness is desirable in the sense of capable of being desired" is false. Is S3, "In like manner, I apprehend, the sole evidence it is possible to produce that anything is desirable, is that people do actually desire it.", true then?
mill-proof.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: mill-proof.logic.r2 has "desires_own → general_good"; mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "each person, so far as he believes it attainable, desires his own happiness" is true, which the second allows
mill-proof.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered yes, which keeps mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "each person, so far as he believes it attainable, desires his own happiness" is true and "the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of all persons" is false. Is S5, "No reason can be given why the general happiness is desirable, except that each person, so far as he believes it to be attainable, desires his own happiness.", true then?
mill-proof.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: mill-proof.logic.r2 has "own_good → general_good" and "general_good → end_of_conduct" and "end_of_conduct → criterion"; mill-proof.logic.r2.2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "each person's happiness is a good to that person (desirable in the sense of worthy of desire)", "the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of all persons" and "happiness is one of the ends of conduct" are true, which the second allows
mill-proof.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: mill-proof.logic.r2 and mill-proof.logic.r2.2 have "criterion"; mill-proof.logic.r2.3 has "end_of_conduct ∧ criterion"; they differ when only "happiness is one of the criteria of morality" is true, which the first allows
mill-proof.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps mill-proof.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "happiness is one of the criteria of morality" is true and "happiness is one of the ends of conduct" is false. Is the conclusion, "Happiness has made out its title as one of the ends of conduct, and consequently one of the criteria of morality.", true then?
